meta {
  name: Signup reaches the trial-granting write path
  type: http
  seq: 14
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/auth/signup
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "trial-owner@qa-signup.test",
    "password": "{{qaPassword}}",
    "businessName": "QA Trial Signup Co"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.data.userId: isDefined
  res.body.data.tenantId: isDefined
  Object.keys(res.body.data).length: eq 2
  res.body.meta.requestId: isDefined
  res.body.error: isUndefined
}

docs {
  GRAFT-26 AC1, over HTTP — the half a `.bru` can actually prove.

  Amendment 1 on the issue is what this file obeys: it asserts **only** 201 and
  that the envelope carries exactly the two ids. It deliberately does not assert
  trial data on this response. `POST /api/v1/auth/signup` returns
  `{ userId, tenantId }` and nothing else — the shape is fixed in
  src/app/api/v1/auth/signup/route.ts:27, a protected path this contract does
  not touch, and bruno/auth/signup.bru already asserts those are the only two
  keys. Adding fields here would turn that MUST-pass request red.

  So what this proves is that the signup route still succeeds now that
  `signup()` grants a 14-day Premium trial inside the same request — the grant
  is a second write through the billing port, and a failure in it deletes the
  tenant and surfaces as a 5xx rather than this 201. The trial's *content* is
  proven where it is readable: accounts.test.ts / accounts.integration.test.ts
  on the documents, and bruno/billing/trial-days-remaining.bru on GET /me.

  Single-shot against a freshly seeded QA stack, like signup.bru: the email is
  fixed so the assertions can be, and `npm run qa:db:down` destroys the database
  between runs (docs/WORKFLOW.md §5.2). Run twice without reseeding and it
  returns 409, which is correct behaviour, not a flake.

  Every assertion is in the synchronous `assert` block on purpose — Bruno's CLI
  runs an async `test()` body but does not count it toward pass/fail.
}
